# Glomerulonephritis and Acute Nephritic Syndrome

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Glomerular haematuria</u> - presence of RBC in urine that is of glomerular origin, characterised by presence of dysmorphic RBCs w/ or w/o RBC casts
  - <u>Nephritic syndrome</u> - presentation of glomerulonephritis, that is characterised by glomerular inflammation resulting in 1) haematuria, 2) varying degree of proteinuria, 3) accelerating hypertension, 4) renal impairments, which may reflect a localised or systemic pathology
  - <u>Isolated glomerular haematuria</u> - presence of RBC in urine of glomerular origin w/ absence of proteinuria, elevated BP or renal impairments
- **Modes of presentation of glomerulonephritis:**
  - <u>Chronic kidney disease</u> - often a slow and indolent rise of SCr and proteinuria that is asymptomatic
  - <u>Acute, transient course</u> - e.g. as isolated glomerular haematuria, or nephritic syndrome
  - <u>Acute, fulminant course w/ progressively deteriorating kidney function</u> - rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis
- **DDx of suspected glomerulonephritis:** ![](../img/major_pathological_types_of_gn.png) 
![](../img/ddx_of_glomerulonephritis.png)
- **Clinical assessment** - certain clinical findings suggests specific etiologies of GN:
  - <u>Association w/ upper respiratory tract infection</u>:
    - Latent period of 7-10d between infection and gross haematuria is suggestive of PSGN
    - Gross haematuria concurrent with ongoing infection (i.e. synpharyngitic GN) is typical of IgA nephropathy
  - <u>Skin lesions</u> - palpable purpura or petechial rash is suggestive of underlying small vessel vasculitis (e.g. ANCA-associated vasculitis, Henoch-Schonlein purpura, cryoglobulinaemia)
  - <u>Pulmonary involvement</u> - haemoptysis or pulmonary haemorrhage is suggestive of Anti-GBM disease
  - <u>Rheumatological involvement</u> (e.g. arthritis) - suggestive of autoimmune disease and lupus
- **Ix:**
  - **Urine:**
    - <u>Dipstick</u> - for protein, blood, WBC (leukocyturia in absence of UTI)
    - <u>Microscopy</u> - to confirm glomerular haematuria
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, ESR/CRP, LDH, uric acid:
    - <u>CBC</u>:
      - Sudden drop of Hb may suggest pulmonary haemorrhage
      - More commonly, NcNc anaemia may suggest anaemia of renal disease or an underlying systemic illness
      - Presence of **thrombocytopenia**, and **macrocytic anaemia w/ high retic count** (i.e. MAHA) should <u>immediately raise suspicion of TMA</u>
      - Eosinophilia may be suggestive of Churg-Strauss Syndrome
    - <u>LFT</u>:
      - Bilirubin to detect any MAHA
      - Reverse AG ratio suggestive of a systemic autoimmune phenomenon
      - Detection of hypoalbuminaemia
    - <u>RFT</u> - characterise any renal derrangement (compare w/ previous studies to see whether acute)
    - <u>ESR/CRP</u> - presence of active inflammation
    - <u>LDH, uric acid</u> - haemolysis (+/- reduced haptoglobin to confirm intravascular haemolysis)
  - **CXR:**
    - <u>Features of volume overload</u> - e.g. Kerley B lines, Alveolar oedema
    - <u>Batwing appearance</u> - may be associated w/ volume overload, but raises suspicion of **pulmonary haemorrhage** if a/w low Hb and normal heart size
    - <u>Reticulonodular shadowing</u> - fibrotic changes in systemic inflammatory disease w/ lung and kidney involvements
  - **Laboratory test for specific cause:**
    - <u>Serology for autoimmune diseases</u>:
      - ANA and anti-dsDNA - high titres suggestive of lupus nephritis
      - C3/4 - good for differentiating cause (low in immune-complex mediated GN, normal in anti-GBM or pauci-immune disease)
      - Anti-GBM - suggestive of anti-GBM disease
      - ANCA - for vasculitis
    - <u>Serology for infections</u> - ASLO, HCV, HBV, HIV
    - <u>Ix for monoclonal gammopathies</u> - SPE w/ immunofixation + SFLC assay
  - **Renal Bx** - usually pursued unless C/I
